50
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:17,300
Speaker 3: So take a listen.

51
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:22,080
Speaker 1: Hello, and welcome to a live episode of the Odd Lots podcast.

52
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:23,000
Speaker 1: I'm Joe Wiesenthal.

53
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:24,240
Speaker 3: And I'm Tracey Alloway.

54
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:31,540
Speaker 1: And we are joined here by Tim Latimer, co-founder and CEO of Fervo Energy Advanced Geothermal.

55
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:32,520
Speaker 1: What is that?

56
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:40,920
Speaker 1: And when we think about America's energy mix or America's energy portfolio, how big can advanced geothermal be?

57
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:42,040
Speaker 0: Very big.

58
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:43,560
Speaker 0: That's the short answer.

59
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:45,380
Speaker 0: So first off, great to be with you all.

60
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:48,600
Speaker 0: I'm a regular Odd Lots listener, so it's a treat to be with you all.

61
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:49,740
Speaker 0: Geothermal.

62
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:51,780
Speaker 0: What is geothermal and what is advanced geothermal?

63
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:52,640
Speaker 0: It's a great question.

64
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:57,080
Speaker 0: So geothermal isn't new and even power generation from geothermal isn't new.

65
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:01,760
Speaker 0: The first power plant for geothermal came online actually over 100 years ago in Italy.

66
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:04,700
Speaker 0: So it's been something that's been around for a long time.

67
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:11,380
Speaker 0: But historically, even though it has really everything you want in an energy resource, it's carbon free, low environmental footprint.

68
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:13,280
Speaker 0: It works 24-7.

69
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:15,980
Speaker 0: It's been very limited to very specific geology.

70
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:29,860
Speaker 0: So up until very recently, the story of geothermal is you either have the geology of Iceland, where steam is literally coming out of the ground, or certain hotspots like Northern California or New Zealand, or you don't have geothermal, and those were kind of the only two options.

71
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:34,740
Speaker 0: So those places blessed with it had a great energy resource, but it certainly wasn't widespread.

72
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:40,880
Speaker 0: Advanced geothermal or enhanced geothermal systems is really the whole concept that you could use new technology.

73
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:47,500
Speaker 0: to go to deeper resources or impermeable rock where the hot water or the steam wouldn't flow on its own and still make geothermal work.

74
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:51,160
Speaker 0: And so in enhanced geothermal systems, oftentimes you're going deeper.

75
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:56,940
Speaker 0: In many cases, you're doing something to create permeability in the reservoir so it can work in more places.

76
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:03,580
Speaker 0: And the key there is you get all the positive attributes of geothermal, but you're not limited to just doing it in places like Iceland's geology.

77
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:05,120
Speaker 0: So the opportunity is huge.

78
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:06,020
Speaker 0: In the U.S.

79
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:14,120
Speaker 0: today, geothermal only accounts for about three gigawatts of power, so it'll leave less than 1% of the US electric grid, but the resource potential is massive.

80
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:22,940
Speaker 0: You know, we just worked on a paper that was published as a collaboration with Princeton that actually showed pathways to get up to 750 gigawatts of geothermal power by mid-century.

81
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:26,660
Speaker 0: So if we unlock it with the right technology, this could be an enormous resource.

82
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:28,820
Speaker 3: So you're a fracking guy.

83
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:40,900
Speaker 3: What's the technological overlap between fracking for oil and gas in like the Permian Basin or somewhere like that versus doing fracking for geothermal in Utah or something like that?

84
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:49,360
Speaker 3: And I have to admit here, my only knowledge of drilling comes from like Bruce Willis in Armageddon, which I assume was an accurate depiction of the industry.

85
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:50,300
Speaker 3: But you tell me.

86
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:51,040
Speaker 0: That's right.

87
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:54,240
Speaker 0: Yeah, Armageddon, actually, I thought it was a documentary the first time I saw it.

88
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:54,900
Speaker 0: Great.

89
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:59,420
Speaker 0: And you're right, I actually started my career as a drilling engineer in the oil and gas industry.

90
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:03,140
Speaker 0: And there's a lot of technology overlap between oil and gas and geothermal.

91
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:11,700
Speaker 0: And that's why the shale revolution and all the technological development that's happened as a result of that has had spillover effects that have moved the needle on geothermal technology.

92
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:13,180
Speaker 0: But there's also a lot that's different.

93
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:19,500
Speaker 0: And actually, the origins of Fervo, I got the ideas for this when I was drilling in the Eagleford Shale in South Texas.

94
00:06:19,780 --> 00:06:27,720
Speaker 0: And the Eagleford had been a resource that people had known about for over 100 years, but it lacked the permeability to produce economic quantities of oil.

95
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:30,600
Speaker 0: And so it was ignored really until the late 2000s.

96
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:42,980
Speaker 0: And really it was the process of horizontal drilling where you could drill deep and then horizontally across the shale layer and the hydraulic fracturing process to create permeability that allowed those wells to be economic and kind of launch the shale boom.

97
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:46,720
Speaker 0: And I was looking at what I was doing in my career as a drilling engineer there.

98
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:58,520
Speaker 0: And I learned what geothermal was at the time, and I got really excited because I realized that the technological gap of getting high flow rates out of low permeability formations really was the exact same whenever you looked at geothermal.

99
00:06:58,600 --> 00:06:59,820
Speaker 0: That's what held shale back.

100
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:01,360
Speaker 0: That's what was holding geothermal back.

101
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:06,220
Speaker 0: And the fact that these new technologies had opened up oil and gas meant it could be done for geothermal too.

102
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:15,320
Speaker 0: But the process of taking that technique and applying it to geothermal has involved an enormous amount of technical work because there's a lot different between oil and gas and geothermal.

103
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:19,320
Speaker 0: The first thing with probably pretty obvious is we look for much higher temperatures.

104
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:28,380
Speaker 0: So, you know, the Eagle Ford, when I was drilling in South Texas, is actually considered very hot for an oil and gas basin onshore, but it's only like 300 degrees Fahrenheit.

105
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:30,180
Speaker 0: That's considered cold for geothermal.

106
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:35,020
Speaker 0: And so we needed to figure out how to develop the tools and techniques to go much higher temperature than that.

107
00:07:35,420 --> 00:07:41,800
Speaker 0: We're now doing products up to 440 degrees Fahrenheit in our drilling, and that has taken an enormous amount of tech development to make that happen.

108
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:49,520
Speaker 0: And it also turns out that the places that are hot geologically overlap very often with granite, which is a much harder rock type than shale.

109
00:07:49,980 --> 00:08:00,260
Speaker 0: So in order to take these technologies from oil and gas and make them work in geothermal, we had to develop techniques to deal with the fact that the geology is different and it's much harder, oftentimes a much more challenging engineering environment.

110
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:08,040
Speaker 0: And so our company has been around for seven years now and a lot of that work has been trying to figure out what are now solved problems in oil and gas.

111
00:08:08,260 --> 00:08:08,880
Speaker 0: How do you solve them?

112
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:15,440
Speaker 0: in a completely different context oftentimes where the rock is harder and the temperatures are hotter and the operation of the reservoir is very different?

113
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:22,460
Speaker 1: What do you have today in operation such that you can say these tech problems are provably solved?

114
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:25,260
Speaker 1: Because obviously, as you think about the future, there are many things.

115
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:26,860
Speaker 1: You have to get the financing right.

116
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:29,240
Speaker 1: You have to get the customers lined up.

117
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:30,440
Speaker 1: You have to figure out how to scale it.

118
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:39,740
Speaker 1: But just in terms of like, OK, where we are in December of 2024, what can you show us if we were on a site that say, OK, we've shown that we can solve the tech side?

119
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:45,020
Speaker 0: So the gold standard for new tech deployment is actually an operating project, right?

120
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:56,820
Speaker 0: So since this is a deployed tech-focused conference, I'll say TRL9, Technology Readiness Level 9, the final hurdle along that way, which is actually the project working in its intended environment and operating conditions.

121
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,560
Speaker 0: And that's something we achieved with Enhanced Geothermal Systems actually just over a year ago when we brought our first project online.

122
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:08,640
Speaker 0: So going back to 2021, we created a partnership with Google as part of their 24-7 carbon-free energy initiative.

123
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:21,520
Speaker 0: And we actually selected a site in northern Nevada where there was an existing producing geothermal power plant there, but it had been troubled by the same challenges that had held back geothermal for decades, principally what we call dry hole risk.

124
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:22,980
Speaker 0: They'd actually drilled a bunch of wells.

125
00:09:23,400 --> 00:09:24,580
Speaker 0: Some of the wells were successful.

126
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:25,620
Speaker 0: A lot of the wells weren't.

127
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:30,400
Speaker 0: And so as a result, the power plant wasn't producing as much as it could based off its capacity.

128
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:37,720
Speaker 0: So we actually partnered with the operator there, and we drilled a set of new wells, including the first two horizontal well pair for geothermal.

129
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:43,220
Speaker 0: And we actually created that project and brought that power online in late 2023.

130
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:45,660
Speaker 0: And so we really needed to prove a few things.

131
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:49,480
Speaker 0: One, that we can actually drill and deliver those wells, even though it was through granite and much higher temperature.

132
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,820
Speaker 0: Two, that we could get highly productive flow rates out of those wells.

133
00:09:53,260 --> 00:10:00,940
Speaker 0: And the important one, and the one that's harder to prove because it takes time, is that the temperature profile and the production profile of those wells would stay constant over time.

134
00:10:01,380 --> 00:10:17,580
Speaker 0: So one of the really exciting things for Fervo, in October, we actually hit the 12-month mark, where not only did we achieve all the technical objectives of the project, but it was actually producing geothermal energy connected to the grid, producing electrons for over a year and we saw no evidence of production decline in that year.

135
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:21,280
Speaker 0: So we can say with a lot of confidence now this is a completely proven technology.

136
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:23,200
Speaker 0: Mainly because we've actually done it.

137
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:25,500
Speaker 0: We have a product that's producing electricity for the grid today.

138
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:28,040
Speaker 3: So what's your funding mix like at the moment?

139
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:39,580
Speaker 3: Because I imagine part of the reason you want a working project that you can show everyone is so that you can issue more debt, like sort of more traditional energy companies, older energy companies, and maybe move away from equity.

140
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:41,720
Speaker 0: Yes, that's exactly right.

141
00:10:41,820 --> 00:10:46,440
Speaker 0: And we've had an interesting funding journey that's probably very similar to many of the companies here at Deploy.

142
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:50,900
Speaker 0: You know, early on, we were backed from many grant contributions from the Department of Energy.

143
00:10:51,060 --> 00:10:54,680
Speaker 0: We partnered on many aspects of new geothermal technology development.

144
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:58,220
Speaker 0: We actually were part of the Activate program at the Lawrence Berkeley National Lab.

145
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:04,260
Speaker 0: So we were back in 2018, got kind of our start through some DOE research programs and grant funding.

146
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:06,920
Speaker 0: And on the private side, it was really venture capital funding.

147
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:21,040
Speaker 0: So funds like Breakthrough Energy Ventures and Congruent Ventures stepped in and got us up early on, you know, those funds that specialized in looking at companies that were so early in their development that it was really just an idea or a PowerPoint slide, but would sit in and understand that technology with you.

148
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:25,060
Speaker 0: And then as we expanded, what we've brought in is a lot more institutional investors.

149
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:28,100
Speaker 0: We've actually gotten investment from the oil and gas industry now.

150
00:11:28,540 --> 00:11:31,560
Speaker 0: We raised the Series D round in February of this year.

151
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:40,280
Speaker 0: That was a $244 million Series D, and that was actually led by Devon Energy, one of the leading independent oil and gas producers out of Oklahoma City with $100 million investment.

152
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:42,160
Speaker 0: So the investment mix has changed over time.

153
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:52,600
Speaker 0: But for a lot of new technologies, there's this crossing the chasm level where, you know, a venture capitalist or a DOE grant writer will sit with you and understand the ins and outs of your technology.

154
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:57,220
Speaker 0: And they'll see is this feasible or not, and you can actually have a good conversation around that.

155
00:11:57,680 --> 00:11:59,460
Speaker 0: Institutional money that wants to do project debt.

156
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:01,980
Speaker 0: They don't really want to understand how your technology works.

157
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:04,540
Speaker 0: They just want to see that you've done it many, many times before.

158
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:07,880
Speaker 0: And that kind of gets to the chicken and egg problem that's with this funding.

159
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:17,920
Speaker 0: You know, so often when we approach those larger infrastructure sources of funding, the answer is we'll bring us 12 months of data or bring us an independent engineering report that says 0% chance.

160
00:12:17,960 --> 00:12:19,080
Speaker 0: this will ever, ever fail.

161
00:12:19,640 --> 00:12:21,440
Speaker 0: And that's kind of a big hurdle to jump.

162
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:26,820
Speaker 0: So we finally crossed that chasm earlier this year because we brought that project online.

163
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:29,020
Speaker 0: We announced in July a hundred million dollar.

164
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:33,480
Speaker 0: from Excalibur Rural Capital, that was the first time we brought in project finance.

165
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:41,680
Speaker 0: So we're finally moving away from the expensive venture capital sources of funding and down into the more sustainable long-term infrastructure style of funding.

166
00:12:41,740 --> 00:12:50,860
Speaker 0: But it took us seven years from inception, an operating fully successful project, and a lot more work to finally cross over that funding mix barrier.

167
