Speaker 0: Let's create.

182
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:12,340
Speaker 1: So obviously we want to talk more about the chicken and egg problem because in a way I mean that's the entire premise of all of this.

183
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:31,840
Speaker 1: But just before we dive more into that, you know You mentioned shale and there is this story that people tell and I only maybe like 50% believe it or 60% believe it that the shale boom was like partly a tech story and partly a low interest rate story or that a funding story and There are various theories about how the Fed helped cause the shale boom again.

184
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:33,220
Speaker 1: Not totally sure if I buy it.

185
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:44,820
Speaker 1: that being said For any sort of renewable or clean energy project or anything that has a lot of upfront capital costs, the rate conditions in the beginning matter quite a bit to the math.

186
00:14:44,980 --> 00:14:50,800
Speaker 1: And I imagine here in 2024 versus when you're seven years ago, that picture looks very different.

187
00:14:51,140 --> 00:14:58,720
Speaker 1: Talk to us about the sort of, I guess, mathematical implications of where we are with interest rates in terms of making geothermal projects pencil out.

188
00:14:58,820 --> 00:15:00,180
Speaker 0: Yeah, it's a great question.

189
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:09,620
Speaker 0: And the fact that we've gone through such a big movement in interest rates over the last five years has meant we've kind of gone through multiple business cycles in this sector in a very compressed period of time.

190
00:15:10,280 --> 00:15:17,980
Speaker 0: As interest rates started to rise a couple of years ago, there were two sectors or areas that sort of bubbled to the surface as being challenged by a high interest rate environment.

191
00:15:18,060 --> 00:15:26,340
Speaker 0: That was, number one, startups that were based on very long-term growth, and number two, capital-intensive businesses, where it's a lot of capital upfront with a long payback period.

192
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:30,040
Speaker 0: That was a double whammy because we were running a capital intensive startup.

193
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:36,340
Speaker 0: And so the interest rate environment was very challenging to try to get new capital to come into the space as those interest rates went up.

194
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:46,140
Speaker 0: And, you know, one of the things about renewable energy, and this is true about solar and wind, but also true about geothermal, is these are oftentimes high capex but long payback period projects.

195
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:51,940
Speaker 0: And they're also projects that are interesting because you end up with a different profile than a lot of other energy infrastructure projects.

196
00:15:52,020 --> 00:15:56,860
Speaker 0: Because, you know, all of our projects we build, we have 15-year off-take agreements with locked-in pricing.

197
00:15:57,220 --> 00:16:01,660
Speaker 0: So we don't have to worry five years from now what, you know, is the price of our product going to go up or down.

198
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:03,700
Speaker 0: We also don't have any fuel cost.

199
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:06,940
Speaker 0: And so we're not exposed if there's a big run up in fuel costs.

200
00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:08,980
Speaker 0: And so we eliminate a lot of that volatility.

201
00:16:09,140 --> 00:16:20,340
Speaker 0: So as a result, renewable energy projects like geothermal have always been viewed as historically much safer investments because you're not exposed to a lot of the volatility mix that impact other infrastructure investments.

202
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:26,000
Speaker 0: That's a good thing in general because it means you can bring low cost capital in the space because it's perceived as safer investments.

203
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:34,060
Speaker 0: But it also means your spread off the risk free rate is a lot tighter than if you were talking about a fossil fuel project or a project with more risk in it.

204
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:39,580
Speaker 0: And so when those rates move the sort of relative impact for something with a low spread is a lot bigger.

205
00:16:39,940 --> 00:16:47,420
Speaker 0: And so the interest rate environment, it hits startups and it hits infrastructure projects, particularly renewable infrastructure projects in a big way.

206
00:16:47,620 --> 00:16:49,760
Speaker 0: And that's one of the things that companies are navigating now.

207
00:16:50,180 --> 00:16:51,540
Speaker 0: There's so many tailwinds.

208
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:53,780
Speaker 0: looking for more and more power.

209
00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:54,820
Speaker 0: We need more reliable power.

210
00:16:54,880 --> 00:16:55,980
Speaker 0: We need more power, period.

211
00:16:56,340 --> 00:17:04,079
Speaker 0: Everything from data centers to industrialization to onshore air manufacturing to, I mean, the AI world is what's driving so many of our conversations right now.

212
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:14,319
Speaker 0: There's all these tailwinds helping the sector out, but the headwind, that interest rate environment is still something that's limiting both deployments of new innovation and also broadly the renewable sector overall.

213
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:20,160
Speaker 3: I mean, presumably this is when someone like the DOE could step in and potentially help.

214
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:23,960
Speaker 3: What kind of support have you seen from the DOE so far?

215
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:26,579
Speaker 0: Yeah, so we've gotten great support from the DOE.

216
00:17:26,720 --> 00:17:34,340
Speaker 0: As I mentioned, we've gotten multiple grants over the years to work on anything from high temperature tools to novel data sensing methods.

217
00:17:34,780 --> 00:17:43,780
Speaker 0: Earlier this year, you know, there was a provision in the bipartisan infrastructure law where there was $84 million earmarked for geothermal energy development.

218
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:49,160
Speaker 0: Fervo was actually the recipient of the largest grant of that, which was a $25 million grant.

219
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:54,180
Speaker 0: And so that kind of early demonstration funding can be very critical for a technology that's taking off.

220
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:55,680
Speaker 0: I don't want to make any enemies.

221
00:17:55,740 --> 00:17:58,400
Speaker 0: I know there's a lot of different technologies out in the audience here.

222
00:17:58,500 --> 00:18:15,860
Speaker 0: But if you read the Department of Energy's commercial liftoff report for geothermal, they've got a nice chart in there where it almost looks like a data error because they put the $84 million that geothermal got up against the $10 billion for nuclear and the $9 billion for carbon capture and the several billions for long duration energy storage and other things.

223
00:18:15,900 --> 00:18:19,400
Speaker 0: So I think other technologies benefited far more than geothermal did.

224
00:18:19,700 --> 00:18:23,320
Speaker 0: But even that kind of move was sort of catalytic to get things off the ground.

225
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:27,820
Speaker 0: And then, of course, the DOE has a big message and a big audience that reads these things.

226
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:41,100
Speaker 0: Actually, this liftoff report that came out earlier this year changed a lot of people's minds because one of the problems with the chicken and egg problem is there's so many investor meetings that we don't even get to have because we reach out and they say, geothermal?

227
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:42,060
Speaker 0: I know geothermal.

228
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:43,020
Speaker 0: We're not looking at that.

229
00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:44,660
Speaker 0: And the meeting never even happens.

230
00:18:44,820 --> 00:18:49,020
Speaker 0: And you have something like the geothermal liftoff report come out that's got the DOE logo on it.

231
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:51,220
Speaker 0: They know it's been well vetted, well researched work.

232
00:18:51,640 --> 00:18:56,300
Speaker 0: And all of a sudden, it may be there's some investors who may have written off geothermal that say, well, maybe there's a new shot here.

233
00:18:56,380 --> 00:18:58,180
Speaker 0: Maybe there's new data that we weren't aware of.

234
00:18:58,260 --> 00:19:01,860
Speaker 0: And so that kind of awareness and technology validation is pretty critical as well.

235
00:19:02,440 --> 00:19:08,380
Speaker 1: So one of the obviously nice things about your technology is that it's clean, it's carbon free.

236
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:10,980
Speaker 1: There are some people that don't care about that particularly much.

237
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:12,900
Speaker 1: They don't rank that particularly highly.

238
00:19:12,940 --> 00:19:16,460
Speaker 1: You know, for some people the biggest priority is cheap energy.

239
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:30,780
Speaker 1: When you think about the future, how important is it that either the public sector or private sector actors who've made various commitments over the years about net zero, other sounding things, How important is it that those priorities stay firm?

240
00:19:31,260 --> 00:19:37,080
Speaker 1: Or if they're sort of changing moods, where in the private sector, this is not as important to us as it was a few years ago?

241
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:38,820
Speaker 1: How does that affect your outlook?

242
00:19:39,480 --> 00:19:40,780
Speaker 0: Yeah, it's a good question.

243
00:19:41,020 --> 00:19:49,760
Speaker 0: And honestly, my view on this is I don't think people actually, there's not really that much of a divide, I think, in what people actually want out of their energy resources.

244
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:51,240
Speaker 0: I think it can get politicized.

245
00:19:51,280 --> 00:19:57,480
Speaker 0: And there's a lot of food fights and political fights in this, but people want three things out of their energy resources.

246
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:02,540
Speaker 0: They want the energy to be cleaner, they want the energy to be more affordable, and they want the energy to be more reliable.

247
00:20:03,460 --> 00:20:05,280
Speaker 1: Does everyone really want it to be cleaner?

248
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:10,720
Speaker 1: I mean, just to be serious, because I get totally cheaper and reliable seem unambiguous.

249
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:15,440
Speaker 1: When it comes to cleaner, I could see if I were anyone.

250
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:16,780
Speaker 1: I don't know.

251
00:20:16,960 --> 00:20:17,960
Speaker 1: It's less important.

252
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:20,460
Speaker 0: I do think everybody wants it to be cleaner.

253
00:20:20,540 --> 00:20:21,160
Speaker 0: And that's true.

254
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:24,120
Speaker 0: If we talk to people on both sides of the aisle, I don't think it matters where it is.

255
00:20:24,440 --> 00:20:30,300
Speaker 0: I think where a lot of the debates come up is where is the trade off between clean and affordable and reliable?

256
00:20:30,380 --> 00:20:36,080
Speaker 0: But I think you'd be hard pressed to find anyone other than maybe some very French people who are just trying to instigate.

257
00:20:36,120 --> 00:20:37,500
Speaker 3: Joe, you stand alone on that one.

258
00:20:38,520 --> 00:20:39,740
Speaker 1: I don't have an opinion here.

259
00:20:40,540 --> 00:20:41,100
Speaker 0: No opinion.

260
00:20:41,400 --> 00:20:43,260
Speaker 0: You're the one out there that doesn't want cleaner energy.

261
00:20:43,420 --> 00:20:44,000
Speaker 1: I'm a journalist.

262
00:20:44,120 --> 00:20:44,880
Speaker 0: All right, journalist.

263
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:45,360
Speaker 0: I like it.

264
00:20:45,800 --> 00:20:52,880
Speaker 0: But I think that what happens is that depending on where people are coming from, you know, which side of the aisle they're on, what their priorities are, they may rank those three things differently.

265
00:20:52,980 --> 00:20:56,240
Speaker 0: And then there's a lot in the macro environment where those things get ranked differently.

266
00:20:56,420 --> 00:21:00,780
Speaker 0: Like very clearly, affordability is top of mind for everyone right now.

267
00:21:01,020 --> 00:21:06,200
Speaker 0: And so What you see is over time, depending on what's going on in the geopolitical environment, the macro environment.

268
00:21:06,520 --> 00:21:09,220
Speaker 0: the political environment, those orders may get flipped.

269
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:16,600
Speaker 0: But I think what most people here are working on is something that is cleaner, is reliable, and is more affordable.

270
00:21:16,720 --> 00:21:25,200
Speaker 0: And the thing is, it's not going to get there overnight, but everybody's working on technologies that are on a path to achieve one of those three things on a better dimension.

271
00:21:25,320 --> 00:21:28,580
Speaker 0: And I think one of the things about geothermal is we emphasize it checks all three boxes.

272
00:21:28,980 --> 00:21:36,900
Speaker 0: It's cleaner, it's reliable, and we're on a cost trajectory where this is going to become one of the most affordable ways to get energy in the near future.

273
00:21:36,980 --> 00:21:41,740
Speaker 0: It doesn't matter if you're talking clean or dirty or intermittent or base load or whatever jargon you want to put on it.

274
00:21:41,820 --> 00:21:47,440
Speaker 0: Geothermal, the cost trajectory is moving so quickly that we're going to get a great answer on the affordability very, very soon.

275
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:55,700
Speaker 0: And so I think emphasizing that we do those three things and it doesn't matter what is going on in the macro environment, those three things are still the things.

276
00:21:55,740 --> 00:21:58,520
Speaker 0: that matter is kind of how we stay resilient through the different cycles.

277
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:01,060
Speaker 3: So you make a very convincing pitch.

278
00:22:01,620 --> 00:22:15,760
Speaker 3: And I guess I'm kind of wondering when you go to talk to potential offtake customers and you say, well, we have cheap, clean and reliable energy that ticks all the boxes, do they immediately jump and go like, yes, we'll take it?

279
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:21,200
Speaker 3: I imagine That can't be the case because this is still new technology.

280
00:22:21,420 --> 00:22:26,520
Speaker 3: They probably presumably don't have geologists on staff who can review all this stuff.

281
00:22:26,660 --> 00:22:32,540
Speaker 3: What are those conversations actually like and what are, I guess, the sticking points to everyone becoming your customer?

282
00:22:33,100 --> 00:22:33,260
Speaker 0: Yeah.

283
00:22:33,740 --> 00:22:37,320
Speaker 0: I mentioned this a little bit before where it's a chicken and egg problem.

284
00:22:37,860 --> 00:22:50,800
Speaker 0: In a lot of ways, entrepreneurship, particularly if you're doing a real hard tech development like what we're doing at Fervo, is trying to solve dozens of simultaneous chicken and egg problems all at once, you know, because you've got to get the finances to believe in it.

285
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:52,320
Speaker 0: You've got to get the customers to believe in it.

286
00:22:52,780 --> 00:22:53,780
Speaker 0: And it's fascinating.

287
00:22:53,860 --> 00:22:54,820
Speaker 0: I can talk through our journey.

288
00:22:54,860 --> 00:23:00,300
Speaker 0: You know, our first offtake agreement was with Google, and that went back all the way into 2021 that we announced that deal.

289
00:23:00,580 --> 00:23:03,040
Speaker 0: And it wasn't an accident that it was Google that did that.

290
00:23:03,100 --> 00:23:06,160
Speaker 0: You know, they'd put out a white paper in 2018.

291
00:23:06,540 --> 00:23:08,480
Speaker 0: And nobody was paying attention to geothermal back then.

292
00:23:08,540 --> 00:23:10,120
Speaker 0: It was written off as a dead sector.

293
00:23:10,740 --> 00:23:15,160
Speaker 0: But they put out a white paper in 2018 that said they had actually achieved their 100% renewable energy goal.

294
00:23:15,700 --> 00:23:18,660
Speaker 0: And so they were looking at what is the next target we want to get to.

295
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,600
Speaker 0: And they came up with a new concept of 24-7 carbon-free energy, which is actually hourly matching.

296
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:29,320
Speaker 0: The concept of 100% renewable energy, it's more of an accounting exercise where you add up how much energy you use for the year.

297
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:31,440
Speaker 0: You add up how much energy you source for the year.

298
00:23:31,860 --> 00:23:35,920
Speaker 0: And if those numbers are equal at the end of the year, you can consider it 100% renewable energy.

299
00:23:36,440 --> 00:23:39,160
Speaker 0: a much more stricter standard is 24-7 carbon-free energy.

300
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:47,260
Speaker 0: And that's looking at, okay, where I'm actually building these facilities and when they're actually using energy, am I sourcing that electricity from clean areas?

301
00:23:47,700 --> 00:23:54,000
Speaker 0: And what they were making the point on is that wind and solar have been phenomenal technologies to move the future of low carbon forward.

302
00:23:54,460 --> 00:23:59,580
Speaker 0: but that we were going to need a new toolkit from storage to nuclear to hydrogen to geothermal to get there.

303
00:23:59,840 --> 00:24:07,040
Speaker 0: And I can tell you that the biggest point of market validation we had when we were raising our series A was in this 20 page paper that Google wrote.

304
00:24:07,080 --> 00:24:09,880
Speaker 0: They mentioned geothermal one time and we're like, see, we're on the map.

305
00:24:09,960 --> 00:24:10,960
Speaker 0: People are talking about it.

306
00:24:11,420 --> 00:24:14,500
Speaker 0: And actually that caused us to reach out to them when we started this relationship.

307
00:24:14,540 --> 00:24:17,720
Speaker 0: that turned into our first development agreement with Google as an off taker.

308
00:24:17,800 --> 00:24:21,500
Speaker 0: And they've now become a large customer of ours and we've announced several more deals there.

309
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:22,520
Speaker 0: But it was interesting.

310
00:24:22,580 --> 00:24:29,840
Speaker 0: it took a company like Google to get there because, you know, in a lot of ways, the same way that financiers want to see a track record, utilities want to too.

311
00:24:30,020 --> 00:24:31,360
Speaker 0: And I don't fault the utilities for this.

312
00:24:31,420 --> 00:24:36,480
Speaker 0: They have an incredibly important job where they are the ones that are responsible for keeping the lights on.

313
00:24:37,020 --> 00:24:45,780
Speaker 0: And so they need to make sure that if they're going to invest the time to prepare the grid and go through all the work to get an energy resource to come online, that it needs to get there.

314
00:24:46,260 --> 00:24:50,500
Speaker 0: And so most utilities won't even look at something until it has a multi-year development track record.

315
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:52,480
Speaker 0: And the utilities are the big power buyers.

316
00:24:52,740 --> 00:24:54,840
Speaker 0: And so this is why it's a chicken and egg problem.

317
00:24:55,260 --> 00:24:56,560
Speaker 0: How do you get a new tech to market?

318
00:24:57,120 --> 00:25:02,420
Speaker 0: when your customers require a multi-year track record to develop it, but how do you get a multi-year track record until you develop it?

319
00:25:02,540 --> 00:25:20,580
Speaker 0: And so it was a very difficult thing to overcome in the beginning, and you're right, not everybody has geologists sitting around, but a company like Google that has access to the resources and the technical expertise that they had was able to work with us over a multi-year period to learn how our technology worked, get excited about it, and that's what kind of unlocked that first operation for us.

320
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:26,560
Speaker 0: You know, now we've actually announced multiple utility deals because we've sort of crossed that chasm, but it was incredibly difficult.

321
00:25:26,600 --> 00:25:28,040
Speaker 0: to get there in the beginning.

322
00:25:28,820 --> 00:25:43,020
Speaker 1: Part of the premise of the loan programs office I take it is that there's proven technologies that exist in the world but a lot of traditional sources of debt financing do not have the expertise to evaluate them or evaluate the risks.

323
00:25:43,440 --> 00:25:49,460
Speaker 1: What happens today or maybe two years ago or something, because now you're known, you've been around for a while.

324
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:56,580
Speaker 1: You walk into a normal bank, you walk into a Wall Street bank, and you try to explain the case for a project financing for geothermal.

325
00:25:57,160 --> 00:26:00,300
Speaker 1: Does that expertise exist or is it we just don't know about this?

326
00:26:00,340 --> 00:26:03,400
Speaker 1: Like, what happens if you try to walk in the door, pick up a phone call to them?

327
00:26:03,580 --> 00:26:06,780
Speaker 0: You know, now it's changed because the perception of geothermal has changed.

328
00:26:06,900 --> 00:26:09,860
Speaker 0: But I can tell you, you know, anecdotes from my years of this.

329
00:26:10,800 --> 00:26:17,360
Speaker 0: As we move forward, one of three things would happen, actually, as we went and approached investors to talk to them about geothermal.

330
00:26:17,700 --> 00:26:18,480
Speaker 0: One is they would ignore us.

331
00:26:18,700 --> 00:26:25,120
Speaker 0: I actually literally had one time I was at an investor conference and I walked up to somebody who was actually a corporate venture capitalist for a large oil company.

332
00:26:25,700 --> 00:26:28,080
Speaker 0: Told him I was doing geothermal, had a startup doing that.

333
00:26:28,460 --> 00:26:31,800
Speaker 0: And he literally puts his hand up and says, let me stop you right there.

334
00:26:32,100 --> 00:26:33,000
Speaker 0: We've looked at geothermal.

335
00:26:33,300 --> 00:26:33,940
Speaker 0: Not going to work.

336
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:35,120
Speaker 0: Let's save both of our time.

337
00:26:35,160 --> 00:26:35,700
Speaker 0: I'll see you later.

338
00:26:35,940 --> 00:26:42,160
Speaker 3: Wait, how many people think when you say geothermal, it's like putting the heat stuff in your basement?

339
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:44,240
Speaker 0: I'm actually glad that you asked this.

340
00:26:44,320 --> 00:26:44,600
Speaker 0: Yeah.

341
00:26:45,020 --> 00:26:45,720
Speaker 3: Let's be very clear.

342
00:26:45,780 --> 00:26:46,400
Speaker 3: This is different.

343
00:26:46,580 --> 00:26:47,780
Speaker 3: This is deep geothermal.

344
00:26:47,920 --> 00:26:48,540
Speaker 0: I should say this.

345
00:26:49,060 --> 00:26:50,020
Speaker 0: First off, I'm a big fan.

346
00:26:50,260 --> 00:26:56,640
Speaker 0: Geothermal heat pumps are ground source heat pumps, really cool technology, but has Nothing to do with what we do.

347
00:26:57,640 --> 00:27:03,240
Speaker 0: So those are, you know, good for home heating, cooling, or larger buildings, shallower wells where you're using the ground as a heat exchange.

348
00:27:03,640 --> 00:27:06,180
Speaker 0: To be clear, we drill wells that are thousands of feet deep.

349
00:27:06,460 --> 00:27:08,300
Speaker 3: So they won't go in our basements.

350
00:27:08,340 --> 00:27:08,780
Speaker 0: Exactly.

351
00:27:08,840 --> 00:27:10,120
Speaker 0: They're not going to go in your basement.

352
00:27:10,260 --> 00:27:15,480
Speaker 0: And then we reach rock that's 400 degrees Fahrenheit, and then we produce energy from that.

353
00:27:15,600 --> 00:27:25,040
Speaker 0: And what we're doing is actually recirculating that geothermal brine through the reservoir and then capturing the heat at the surface through an organic Rankine cycle system to produce electricity by spinning a turbine.

354
00:27:25,400 --> 00:27:26,980
Speaker 0: And so we're not going to go in your basement.

355
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:31,840
Speaker 0: Because, for example, the project we're building in Utah right now is a 400 megawatt project.

356
00:27:31,960 --> 00:27:36,040
Speaker 0: And this is enough power to power a city-sized energy demand.

357
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:38,220
Speaker 0: So it wouldn't go well in your basement.

358
00:27:38,260 --> 00:27:39,340
Speaker 0: But I'm glad you asked that.

359
00:27:39,740 --> 00:27:42,200
Speaker 0: Because actually, I can tell that the tide is turning.

360
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:47,820
Speaker 0: Because up until maybe a year ago, the most common website request we got was, hey, can you do this in my backyard?

361
00:27:48,600 --> 00:27:50,880
Speaker 0: Because people don't think of geothermal power.

362
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:53,420
Speaker 0: It's a quirk of history that they're named the same way.

363
00:28:08,820 --> 00:28:11,460
Speaker 2: Wells Fargo seeks broad impact in their communities.

364
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:20,760
Speaker 2: They're focused on building a sustainable, inclusive future for all by supporting housing affordability, small business growth, financial health, and other community needs.

365
00:28:21,200 --> 00:28:26,800
Speaker 2: That's why they've donated nearly $2 billion to strengthen local communities over the last five years.

366
00:28:27,400 --> 00:28:29,200
Speaker 2: Wells Fargo, the bank of doing.

367
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:32,040
Speaker 2: See how at wellsfargo.com slash say do.

368
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:38,640
Speaker 2: Wells Fargo's philanthropic support includes contributions from Wells Fargo & Company, Wells Fargo Bank NA, and the Wells Fargo Foundation.

369
00:28:39,700 --> 00:28:42,520
Speaker 0: How do you stay ahead when you're building AI applications?

370
00:28:43,880 --> 00:28:45,120
Speaker 0: Start with a head start.

371
00:28:46,560 --> 00:28:53,940
Speaker 0: IBM's high-performance granite models are optimized for enterprise tasks, like domain-specific processes and workflows.

372
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:57,920
Speaker 0: They're designed so you can do less training and more creating.

373
00:28:59,340 --> 00:29:00,360
Speaker 0: Ready to hit the ground running?

374
00:29:01,480 --> 00:29:03,460
Speaker 0: Get started now at ibm.com.

375
00:29:03,580 --> 00:29:06,800
Speaker 0: slash granite IBM let's create.

376
00:29:09,180 --> 00:29:09,640
Speaker 3: one other thing.

377
00:29:09,700 --> 00:29:12,980
Speaker 3: We definitely wanted to talk to you about is Permitting reform.

378
00:29:13,140 --> 00:29:22,760
Speaker 3: Yeah, so, you know when Donald Trump says he's gonna make everything easier in terms of regulation or when Elon Musk says that nowadays What do they mean exactly?

379
00:29:22,840 --> 00:29:25,560
Speaker 3: What does that mean from your perspective, making things easier?

380
00:29:25,860 --> 00:29:35,000
Speaker 0: Yeah, permitting is definitely the long pole in the tent, not just for geothermal, but transmission, which is really important to hardening the grid and bringing new resources online and a whole host of other things.

381
00:29:35,600 --> 00:29:38,020
Speaker 0: I'm not as much of an expert in those things as I am in geothermal.

382
00:29:38,480 --> 00:29:40,920
Speaker 0: So I can speak to some of the challenges we have in geothermal.

383
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:47,020
Speaker 0: One thing is through, you know, a quirk of history, the best geology for geothermal is located in the western United States.

384
00:29:47,560 --> 00:29:50,780
Speaker 0: The western United States is predominantly owned by the US federal government.

385
00:29:50,900 --> 00:29:51,980
Speaker 0: It's not private landowners.

386
00:29:52,060 --> 00:29:54,080
Speaker 0: It's actually the US government that owns most of the land.

387
00:29:54,520 --> 00:30:03,220
Speaker 0: And so that puts us by default into a federal permitting regime on pretty much every project that we do in a way that isn't true of necessarily other energy resources.

388
00:30:03,400 --> 00:30:08,480
Speaker 0: And so we've had to go through multi-year NEPA approval processes for everything we've developed.

389
00:30:08,840 --> 00:30:14,900
Speaker 0: We also simultaneously have to go through the transmission and interconnection process for that, and there's a whole host of other things that are there as well.

390
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:17,500
Speaker 0: I'll be very clear, I think these are good things.

391
00:30:17,560 --> 00:30:29,700
Speaker 0: We need to be doing projects with the right environmental standards, but whenever you look at how these things have been implemented over time, there's a lot of things that are redundant, or a lot of times there's just offices that are not staffed well, so you submit permits and you just never hear back.

392
00:30:30,640 --> 00:30:35,100
Speaker 0: Typical time for permitting the geothermal project in the western United States is about six to eight years.

393
00:30:35,720 --> 00:30:37,480
Speaker 0: to permit to be able to start the project.

394
00:30:37,640 --> 00:30:43,860
Speaker 0: And so that's not really commensurate with, you know, the data center companies that are banging down our door asking if we can bring in power by next year.

395
00:30:44,060 --> 00:30:47,240
Speaker 0: I say, well, once we get through the eight year permitting process, we can get there.

396
00:30:47,340 --> 00:30:49,180
Speaker 0: So there's things that we're trying to do to improve that.

397
00:30:49,640 --> 00:30:57,320
Speaker 0: One of the funny things about geothermal is even though we're drilling wells, just like the oil and gas industry is, we haven't always benefited from the same permitting.

398
00:30:57,540 --> 00:30:58,780
Speaker 3: Yeah, they have some exclusions.

399
00:30:58,860 --> 00:30:59,340
Speaker 0: That's right.

400
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:11,280
Speaker 0: So in one example, You know, there's certain early activities in the exploration phase or just doing extensions to existing projects that don't really have a huge impact, don't really change the scope of the project itself.

401
00:31:11,740 --> 00:31:27,300
Speaker 0: And so if you were an oil and gas company, there's legislatively developed categorical exclusions where there's a whole category of things that if you're drilling an oil well, you don't have to go through the full process for the environmental reviews for certain kind of redundant routine things.

402
00:31:27,960 --> 00:31:34,180
Speaker 0: The law that was passed in 2005 that granted that didn't just say drilling, it said specifically oil and gas drilling.

403
00:31:34,220 --> 00:31:37,280
Speaker 0: So that's been interpreted to say geothermal is not included in that.

404
00:31:37,380 --> 00:31:51,160
Speaker 0: So there's actually a lot of examples of places where the permitting structure, even though geothermal is a lower impact resource, it's a low no emission resource, it actually has a much more extensive permitting program than a lot of oil and gas doing on federal

405
00:31:51,200 --> 00:31:53,520
Speaker 1: permitting reform bill that's currently being talked about.

406
00:31:53,560 --> 00:31:56,060
Speaker 1: Would that change it to what would be ideal for you?

407
00:31:56,400 --> 00:31:56,460
Speaker 1: Yes.

408
00:31:57,180 --> 00:31:59,700
Speaker 1: Let's say this bill passes at some point, what is the timeline?

409
00:31:59,820 --> 00:32:00,780
Speaker 1: then look for a project?

410
00:32:00,920 --> 00:32:02,680
Speaker 0: Yeah, it would make a big difference.

411
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:04,480
Speaker 0: There's many bills that have passed, actually.

412
00:32:04,660 --> 00:32:06,720
Speaker 0: This is one of our big success cases in geothermal.

413
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:11,080
Speaker 0: I used to joke that we were bipartisan in the sense that both parties ignore geothermal.

414
00:32:11,920 --> 00:32:16,840
Speaker 0: Now I can say we're bipartisan because I think both parties recognize the huge opportunity there is for geothermal.

415
00:32:16,900 --> 00:32:21,060
Speaker 0: So there's actually been five bills passed in the House this year for geothermal permitting reform.

416
00:32:21,260 --> 00:32:27,300
Speaker 0: And the current Manchin-Barasso bill that's in discussion in the Senate has a bunch of provisions in there around geothermal.

417
00:32:27,800 --> 00:32:29,040
Speaker 0: And this is truly bipartisan.

418
00:32:29,100 --> 00:32:33,460
Speaker 0: There's groups on both sides of the aisle that have sponsored these bills and it's gotten great votes.

419
00:32:33,580 --> 00:32:40,820
Speaker 0: And so it depends on which bill we're talking about, but generally it does provide parity on these things and it does actually remove a lot of the redundant permitting.

420
00:32:40,860 --> 00:32:50,660
Speaker 0: And there's been good studies out there by NREL, the National Renewable Energy Laboratory, that says these reforms can probably take the permitting process from six to eight years down to two to four years.

421
00:32:50,940 --> 00:32:53,640
Speaker 0: And so it's not, again, it's not like we're doing these projects with no environmental.

422
00:32:54,480 --> 00:33:00,060
Speaker 0: But I can tell you, when I go out in the market and try to raise capital to do a new project, I say, hey, give me the money today.

423
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:01,440
Speaker 0: We'll start drilling in 2031.

424
00:33:02,380 --> 00:33:05,360
Speaker 0: That's a little bit less of a compelling pitch than give me the money today.

425
00:33:05,400 --> 00:33:05,980
Speaker 1: Meanwhile, you're paying.

426
00:33:06,460 --> 00:33:08,520
Speaker 1: you know, SOFR plus whatever for all those years.

427
00:33:08,580 --> 00:33:09,080
Speaker 1: Exactly.

428
00:33:09,260 --> 00:33:17,720
Speaker 0: So these changes, because it's not just when the projects come online, but it's the investment horizon that you have to burden these projects with.

429
00:33:18,200 --> 00:33:24,420
Speaker 0: Moving it from seven years to three years can make the difference between a project being investable or not being investable.

430
00:33:24,520 --> 00:33:25,780
Speaker 0: So it's a massive difference.

431
00:33:26,480 --> 00:33:33,880
Speaker 3: This might be an unfair question, but if you were going to sort of like choose the biggest impediment to scaling up geothermal at the moment?

432
00:33:34,020 --> 00:33:36,240
Speaker 3: I mean, there's financing, which we talked about.

433
00:33:36,340 --> 00:33:38,340
Speaker 3: There's getting customers on board.

434
00:33:38,420 --> 00:33:41,860
Speaker 3: Presumably there's some aspect of the technology that could be improved as well.

435
00:33:42,660 --> 00:33:43,640
Speaker 3: Give us that hierarchy.

436
00:33:43,760 --> 00:33:46,780
Speaker 3: Like what's number one in terms of impediments?

437
00:33:47,220 --> 00:33:55,440
Speaker 0: Oh, it's a good question because I feel like Over the seven years since we started Fervo, the answer has been different every year, which I think is the right thing.

438
00:33:55,520 --> 00:33:56,380
Speaker 0: There should be an impediment.

439
00:33:56,420 --> 00:33:58,360
Speaker 0: You should be knocking it down and getting on to the next one.

440
00:33:58,420 --> 00:34:01,940
Speaker 0: So the early days were, there's fundamental questions on if this would even work.

441
00:34:02,160 --> 00:34:03,860
Speaker 0: And that took us several years to address.

442
00:34:03,960 --> 00:34:05,140
Speaker 0: And thankfully the answer is yes.

443
00:34:05,240 --> 00:34:15,960
Speaker 0: If you would have asked me a few months ago, I would have said funding was the big barrier because you still ran into that situation where people thought they knew geothermal and thought it was yesterday's news and so didn't even want to look at it.

444
00:34:16,340 --> 00:34:17,560
Speaker 0: But that's shifted dramatically.

445
00:34:17,659 --> 00:34:20,760
Speaker 0: We see a lot of interest from capital providers and the geothermal now.

446
00:34:21,060 --> 00:34:25,219
Speaker 0: And then we've actually had a lot of success on improving the permitting even before these bills pass through.

447
00:34:25,360 --> 00:34:29,900
Speaker 0: So if you would have asked me a few months ago, I would have said transformers and high voltage electrical equipment.

448
00:34:30,199 --> 00:34:31,800
Speaker 1: Yeah, it always comes back to transformers.

449
00:34:31,840 --> 00:34:33,840
Speaker 0: Yeah, it does always come back to transformers.

450
00:34:33,960 --> 00:34:36,639
Speaker 0: I would have said permitting and I would have said funding.

451
00:34:36,739 --> 00:34:39,020
Speaker 0: Funding is becoming a much more solved issue.

452
00:34:39,440 --> 00:34:42,679
Speaker 0: Permitting is something that I think we've made a lot of administrative progress on.

453
00:34:42,719 --> 00:34:44,699
Speaker 0: and we're anticipating legislative progress on.

454
00:34:45,199 --> 00:34:50,520
Speaker 0: So now I think about supply chain, you know, because I think we've got the money, we've got the offtake agreements, we've got everything there.

455
00:34:51,020 --> 00:34:53,600
Speaker 0: You are thinking about how do we get the supply chain in place for this?

456
00:34:54,080 --> 00:34:57,760
Speaker 0: Transformers being a big part of that, but there's also other pieces of equipment that are important as well.

457
00:34:58,180 --> 00:35:01,860
Speaker 3: Joe, it's so crazy that we basically had four years of Transformers shortages.

458
00:35:01,900 --> 00:35:02,280
Speaker 1: That's right.

459
00:35:02,880 --> 00:35:09,020
Speaker 1: As of the most recent ISM manufacturing report, electrical components have been in shortage for 50 straight months.

460
00:35:09,300 --> 00:35:10,680
Speaker 1: So four years and two months.

461
00:35:11,200 --> 00:35:12,780
Speaker 1: I actually would love to talk more about that.

462
00:35:13,060 --> 00:35:20,200
Speaker 1: Just on private sector financing specifically, what is the ecosystem that's emerged that wasn't in place?

463
00:35:20,240 --> 00:35:22,680
Speaker 1: If you said, okay, a year ago or two years ago, that would have been the big thing.

464
00:35:22,960 --> 00:35:27,460
Speaker 1: What does it look like today on the private sector side such that you can raise capital for these projects?

465
00:35:27,600 --> 00:35:28,260
Speaker 0: Yeah, yeah.

466
00:35:28,440 --> 00:35:30,160
Speaker 0: I mean, there's been big changes in the last year or two.

467
00:35:30,220 --> 00:35:42,180
Speaker 0: I think some of that is change in... It's banks, it's private capital, it's really all of the above.

468
00:35:42,380 --> 00:35:46,780
Speaker 0: You know, if you think back... 15 years ago into what they call the clean tech 1.0 bust.

469
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:48,860
Speaker 0: I think that one of the failure modes there was.

470
00:35:48,900 --> 00:35:52,940
Speaker 0: a bunch of venture capital went into technologies that took a long time to develop.

471
00:35:53,020 --> 00:35:58,100
Speaker 0: And there wasn't really a pass the baton of who was the next source of capital that was going to keep these technologies going.

472
00:35:58,540 --> 00:35:59,920
Speaker 0: And that's what really led to the bust.

473
00:36:00,160 --> 00:36:04,540
Speaker 0: But that bust also, I think, created a bunch of people who learned hard lessons about what that was like.

474
00:36:04,620 --> 00:36:07,080
Speaker 0: And a lot of them have gone out and created the ecosystem that's there.

475
00:36:07,320 --> 00:36:16,900
Speaker 0: So, you know, for example, that activate program that was at the Lawrence Berkeley National Lab that let us have two years to get our feet under us before we really got going as a company didn't exist in the first one.

476
00:36:16,940 --> 00:36:17,940
Speaker 0: So that was really important.

477
00:36:18,340 --> 00:36:23,020
Speaker 0: What we've seen as we get bigger and bigger, you know, people have talked about the missing middle or the valley of death.

478
00:36:23,120 --> 00:36:25,560
Speaker 0: And that's one of the things that the LPO is very instrumental in filling.

479
00:36:26,020 --> 00:36:29,480
Speaker 0: But you know, market inefficiencies, if there's money to be made, don't last for long.

480
00:36:29,580 --> 00:36:30,900
Speaker 0: And so what we're seeing right now.

481
00:36:31,540 --> 00:36:46,140
Speaker 0: is there's a bunch of venture capitalists who historically would have only played in the series ABC range, who've recognized that there's a capital gap for second of a kind, third of a kind deployment, and a lot of them have raised continuity or growth funds to piggyback off their successful companies.

482
00:36:46,620 --> 00:37:02,520
Speaker 0: Meanwhile, those like Traditional companies that infrastructure investors that only wanted to do the 10th project realized that if they actually wanted to sell their LPs on getting a differentiated return, they needed to do something that was differentiating and step into projects earlier.

483
00:37:02,640 --> 00:37:05,980
Speaker 0: So a lot of them have raised funds that will come in one click earlier.

484
00:37:06,200 --> 00:37:14,880
Speaker 0: And so that valley of death is closing from both sides because you're seeing traditional private equity and growth equity and infrastructure investors create new vehicles to come in one click earlier.

485
00:37:14,940 --> 00:37:17,620
Speaker 0: And you're seeing the venture capitalists create continuity investments.

486
00:37:17,760 --> 00:37:19,940
Speaker 0: growth estimates to carry you one click forward.

487
00:37:20,320 --> 00:37:25,700
Speaker 0: And so there used to not really be capital between like the Series B and the infrastructure side.

488
00:37:26,100 --> 00:37:30,340
Speaker 0: And now there's still a gap, but it's getting converged on from both sides.

489
00:37:31,260 --> 00:37:32,960
Speaker 3: I want to talk more about transformers, actually.

490
00:37:33,340 --> 00:37:34,740
Speaker 3: Where are you getting those from?

491
00:37:34,780 --> 00:37:44,080
Speaker 3: Because I imagine as a relatively new company with at least heretofore limited funding, it might be hard to like get on the wait list for this very desirable equipment.

492
00:37:44,480 --> 00:37:54,000
Speaker 3: And I get the sense that big utility providers are probably, you know, number one in the line, or they already have a lot of existing transformers that they can kind of move around.

493
00:37:54,340 --> 00:37:55,660
Speaker 3: How are you actually sourcing those?

494
00:37:55,940 --> 00:37:56,640
Speaker 0: It's a good question.

495
00:37:56,780 --> 00:37:57,220
Speaker 0: And you're right.

496
00:37:57,520 --> 00:38:00,020
Speaker 0: It's highly competitive to get to in the lead times.

497
00:38:00,200 --> 00:38:02,500
Speaker 0: be multi-year, especially for the larger transformers.

498
00:38:03,100 --> 00:38:12,080
Speaker 0: I was in an investment meeting a couple months ago where somebody walked in, very experienced investor, and said, first question out of their gate, have you sourced your GSU transformers yet?

499
00:38:12,140 --> 00:38:13,240
Speaker 0: And we said, yes, we have.

500
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:14,940
Speaker 0: And he says, four out of five times.

501
00:38:14,980 --> 00:38:15,820
Speaker 0: the answer to that is no.

502
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:18,260
Speaker 0: And I don't have to take the rest of the meeting.

503
00:38:18,300 --> 00:38:19,380
Speaker 1: It's a good disciplined investor to ask that question.

504
00:38:19,420 --> 00:38:20,620
Speaker 0: It is a good disciplined investor.

505
00:38:20,660 --> 00:38:21,920
Speaker 0: They knew the right question to ask.

506
00:38:22,080 --> 00:38:24,960
Speaker 0: And it's also an irony, too, because Data centers are growing.

507
00:38:25,040 --> 00:38:26,820
Speaker 0: Everybody's trying to figure out the equipment for that.

508
00:38:26,940 --> 00:38:28,460
Speaker 0: And then everybody's trying to get the grid going.

509
00:38:28,520 --> 00:38:29,900
Speaker 0: Everybody's trying to get the generation going.

510
00:38:30,440 --> 00:38:36,820
Speaker 0: Oftentimes what we hear from the transformer suppliers is the slots are taken by the data center companies that we're trying to sell the power to.

511
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:39,900
Speaker 0: So it's a bit of a circular problem there, which is interesting.

512
00:38:40,080 --> 00:38:42,880
Speaker 0: We didn't have a really creative solution other than ordering early.

513
00:38:42,980 --> 00:38:45,160
Speaker 0: So we found a couple of US-based manufacturers.

514
00:38:45,220 --> 00:38:48,000
Speaker 0: We're sourcing a couple of our transformers from Taiwan as well.

515
00:38:48,560 --> 00:38:52,640
Speaker 0: And there's not really a creative answer other than get in line three or four years in advance.

516
00:38:53,900 --> 00:38:56,640
Speaker 1: manufacturing capacity being built anywhere?

517
00:38:57,240 --> 00:39:06,460
Speaker 0: It's being talked about and this is one that I think is something that I just sort of expected people to look at three, four or five year lead times and say, well, there's money to be made here.

518
00:39:06,520 --> 00:39:07,240
Speaker 0: We're going to expand.

519
00:39:07,700 --> 00:39:11,580
Speaker 0: But I think the transformer companies have gone through a lot of boom and busts before.

520
00:39:12,020 --> 00:39:13,200
Speaker 0: So they're quite reticent there.

521
00:39:13,700 --> 00:39:15,240
Speaker 0: You know, this isn't quite my area of expertise.

522
00:39:15,340 --> 00:39:23,420
Speaker 0: I don't know how many new facilities are being built, but what we're hearing from our suppliers is they're doing the low hanging fruit of adding new lines, adding extra shifts, adding extra hours.

523
00:39:23,820 --> 00:39:26,120
Speaker 0: So you're starting to see the supply come up a little bit.

524
00:39:26,220 --> 00:39:27,920
Speaker 0: But is it going to be enough to meet?

525
00:39:28,420 --> 00:39:33,400
Speaker 0: the unprecedented demand coming from all different sources that need this critical electrical equipment.

526
00:39:33,880 --> 00:39:34,200
Speaker 0: I don't know.

527
00:39:34,440 --> 00:39:36,880
Speaker 0: I still think that's going to be one of the long poles in the tent for a while.

528
00:39:37,440 --> 00:39:48,100
Speaker 3: If Joe and I started making transformers, because it's so easy, could we exchange transformers for equity in up and coming power companies and solve the chicken and egg problem?

529
00:39:48,480 --> 00:39:49,180
Speaker 0: I think you would.

530
00:39:49,560 --> 00:39:50,120
Speaker 0: I think you would.

531
00:39:50,540 --> 00:39:53,300
Speaker 0: When you're running a startup, a lot of times you're trying to solve the problem of the day.

532
00:39:53,820 --> 00:39:56,920
Speaker 0: And you also don't have the balance sheets put down long term orders.

533
00:39:57,820 --> 00:40:00,440
Speaker 0: Not a lot of startups out there that are ordering things four years in advance.

534
00:40:00,520 --> 00:40:02,180
Speaker 0: So definitely a missing part of the market.

535
00:40:02,280 --> 00:40:03,260
Speaker 1: This is a really good idea.

536
00:40:03,500 --> 00:40:04,740
Speaker 1: Yeah, let's do it.

537
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:06,280
Speaker 1: What about the labor component?

538
00:40:06,780 --> 00:40:08,620
Speaker 1: How much skills transfer is there?

539
00:40:08,660 --> 00:40:12,840
Speaker 1: We talked about the tech transfer from shale to geothermal.

540
00:40:13,280 --> 00:40:14,680
Speaker 1: Is there a labor transfer as well?

541
00:40:15,280 --> 00:40:15,520
Speaker 0: Yes.

542
00:40:15,800 --> 00:40:15,940
Speaker 0: Yeah.

543
00:40:16,180 --> 00:40:22,960
Speaker 0: And in fact, just as an example, The drilling rig that we're using for our site right now is a Helmer campaign H&P Flex 3 rig.

544
00:40:23,040 --> 00:40:24,560
Speaker 0: I'm actually wearing H&P socks.

545
00:40:24,600 --> 00:40:25,560
Speaker 0: I noticed your socks.

546
00:40:25,620 --> 00:40:26,780
Speaker 1: Those are really cool socks.

547
00:40:26,880 --> 00:40:27,320
Speaker 0: That's right.

548
00:40:27,440 --> 00:40:29,380
Speaker 0: So they give out great socks.

549
00:40:29,420 --> 00:40:31,080
Speaker 0: So this is actually what the rig looks like.

550
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:31,540
Speaker 0: Do they sell those?

551
00:40:31,580 --> 00:40:32,060
Speaker 0: If you can see.

552
00:40:32,580 --> 00:40:34,620
Speaker 0: No, but if you call them, I'm sure they'll give them to you.

553
00:40:34,800 --> 00:40:38,460
Speaker 0: So yeah, and it's actually the same exact style of drilling rig.

554
00:40:38,500 --> 00:40:41,840
Speaker 0: It's the most technologically sophisticated drilling rig that you can get for onshore drilling.

555
00:40:41,880 --> 00:40:46,580
Speaker 0: And it's the same exact style of drilling rig that I used when I started my career over a decade ago in South Texas.

556
00:40:47,020 --> 00:40:49,920
Speaker 0: And I was insisting on that because I wanted to use the best technology.

557
00:40:50,480 --> 00:40:57,620
Speaker 0: And the crews there, they were just before we picked up that rig a year and a half ago, they were drilling in the Bakken in North Dakota.

558
00:40:58,120 --> 00:41:01,700
Speaker 0: And so people really were drilling oil wells one day and they were drilling geothermal wells the next.

559
00:41:01,740 --> 00:41:04,780
Speaker 0: And there's differences there, but the skills and the workforce is there.

560
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:07,840
Speaker 0: And this is one of the things that I think makes geothermal quite scalable.

561
00:41:08,440 --> 00:41:11,100
Speaker 0: Today there's roughly 600 drilling rigs operating in North America.

562
00:41:11,720 --> 00:41:12,980
Speaker 0: We're growing at a huge rate.

563
00:41:13,180 --> 00:41:16,460
Speaker 0: We're going to bring on a 100 megawatt project in just 18 months from now.

564
00:41:16,840 --> 00:41:18,220
Speaker 0: We're doing that with just one drilling rig.

565
00:41:18,820 --> 00:41:27,000
Speaker 0: And so we can 10X our growth and still only be a single digit percentage of the oil field services market because we have such overlap with them.

566
00:41:27,460 --> 00:41:40,260
Speaker 0: So among the many things that I think has advantaged geothermal among the different options you have for emerging technologies is the fact that there's such a ready supply chain and workforce of skilled people who know how to execute on these projects.

567
00:41:40,800 --> 00:41:52,440
Speaker 0: And we can 10X our growth rate or 100X our growth rate and not run into workforce issues because there's already such a deep bench of technical talent and technician talent that control these wells.

568
00:41:53,200 --> 00:41:59,600
Speaker 3: So if they remade Armageddon, Bruce Willis would be a geothermal driller instead of an oil rig driller.

569
00:41:59,860 --> 00:42:00,280
Speaker 0: That's right.

570
00:42:00,760 --> 00:42:01,140
Speaker 3: Excellent.

